WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.249 --> 00:00:01.040
GOPAL ASHOK: Hello.

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:04.490
I'm Gopal Ashok, product
manager for Cloud Memorystore.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:07.710
Cloud Memorystore is Google
Cloud Platform's in-memory data

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:08.840
store service.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:10.640
The beta release of
Cloud Memorystore

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.590
supports Redis,
which, as you know,

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:15.710
is one of the most popular
in-memory data stores

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:18.050
available today.

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:21.080
Getting started with
Cloud Memorystore is easy.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.470
Once you go to the Google
Cloud console, go to the menu.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:27.570
And if you scroll
down onto Storage,

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:31.650
you will see the
Memorystore option.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.380
If you click on the
Memorystore option,

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.050
it'll take you to the
Memorystore landing page.

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:38.510
If you're creating Memorystore
for the first time,

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:40.430
the Google Cloud console
will automatically

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.140
enable the API for you.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:46.760
In this particular case, I
have created a few instances

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:47.790
already.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.330
But let's go in and
create a new instance.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:54.802
To create a new instance, click
on Create Instance button.

00:00:54.802 --> 00:00:56.510
Once you click on
Create Instance button,

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:58.370
it will take you to the
Create Instance page.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.050
The first thing that
you need to provide

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:02.090
is the instance
ID, which uniquely

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.780
identifies the instance
that you're provisioning.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.670
Once you have provided
the instance ID,

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.020
you can optionally also
provide a display name,

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.485
which is a more descriptive
name for the instance.

00:01:13.485 --> 00:01:15.485
When you provision a Cloud
Memorystore instance,

00:01:15.485 --> 00:01:18.380
we provision Redis version 3.2.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.900
Behind the scenes, we ensure
that all the critical patches

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.300
are applied so that you
don't have to worry about

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:26.720
whether there are any
security vulnerabilities

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.550
that you need to worry about.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:31.970
The next thing you need to
choose is the service tier.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:34.490
Cloud Memorystore provides
due two tiers of service--

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:36.670
basic and standard.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.710
The basic tier provides a
standalone instance of Redis,

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:45.110
and this tier is great
for simple caches.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:47.540
The standard tier provides
a highly-available Redis

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:48.320
instance.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.320
With a highly-available
Redis instance,

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.870
you get both a
master and a replica.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:59.570
Once you select tier,
select the region.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:03.390
With Cloud Memorystore, you can
only connect to the instances

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:04.630
within the same region.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.990
So if you choose US
Central 1, applications

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:10.470
within US Central 1 can connect
to that particular instance.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.600
Once you choose a
region, you can either

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.640
choose a particular
zone where the master

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.850
instance will be deployed,
or you can choose Any.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.980
If you choose Any,
we automatically

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.180
pick the zone for the master
and the replica instance.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:25.860
The next thing
you want to select

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.790
is Instance Capacity or the
size of the Redis instance.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.830
Memorystore allows
you to choose anywhere

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:33.690
between 1 and 300 gigabytes.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:36.970
And depending on the
size, as you can see,

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:39.330
the instance will provide
different network throughput.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.690
For 300 gigabytes, you
can see that you'll

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:44.500
get about 1,500
megabytes per second.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:50.200
And for 1 gigabyte, you get
about 375 megabytes per second.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:54.400
Once you've selected the right
size for your application,

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.190
the next thing
you need to select

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:59.220
is a network, or the VPC
network where the instance is

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.080
going to get deployed.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.900
The VPC network
determines which VMs

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.360
can connect to the instance.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.170
Once you've selected
the network,

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.680
you can also choose
the eviction policy,

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.160
if you choose to change it
from the default. In this case,

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:17.812
I'm going to select allkeys-lru.

00:03:17.812 --> 00:03:19.395
And then you go down
and click Create.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:25.820
Once you click Create, you
can see that the instance

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:27.110
is getting provisioned.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:30.450
But before I tell you how
to connect to the instance,

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.540
I just want to quickly
touch upon how easy it is

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:34.852
to scale a deployed instance.

00:03:34.852 --> 00:03:37.310
Now, if you want to change the
size of a deployed instance,

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:41.340
you click on the instance
and click on the Edit button.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.430
Once you go into the Edit
page, all you need to do

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:44.905
is change the size
of the instance.

00:03:44.905 --> 00:03:47.965
So if you want to change 1 GB
to 5 GB, just change the size

00:03:47.965 --> 00:03:50.390
and click Save, and
we automatically

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:52.010
scale the instance for you.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:55.520
One thing to note here
is that the basic tier--

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.980
when you scale a
basic tier instance,

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:01.580
we fully flush the cache,
and also the application

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:03.050
gets disconnected.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:04.590
But in standard
tier, the scaling

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.380
is mostly an online
operation where

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:09.170
you will lose connectivity
for a brief period,

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:11.270
and there may be a little
bit of data loss because

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:13.650
of unreplicated data.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.260
So as you can see,
it is very easy

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:20.000
to create and scale a Redis
instance using Memorystore.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.180
Now let's take a look at how we
can connect to the Memorystore

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:24.110
instance.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.660
As you can see, when we deploy
the Memorystore instance,

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:29.450
we deploy using a
private IP address.

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:32.870
This is great because your
Redis instance is automatically

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:36.560
protected from the internet,
because it can only be

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.580
connected using a private IP.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:42.980
Cloud Memorystore is also fully
Redis protocol compatible.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:45.870
So you can run Redis
benchmark against it.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:47.180
And let's see how it goes.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.550
As you can see, when
you run the benchmark,

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:52.040
you get pretty good throughput
with the Cloud Memorystore

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.810
instance that you've deployed.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:59.630
So the next obvious question is,
how do I monitor the instance?

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:02.720
To monitor the instance, there
are multiple ways to do it.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.200
If you go into the details of
an instance in Cloud Console,

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.810
there are a few metrics that
we have published that you can

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:11.330
use to monitor the instance.

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.280
But we have more
detail monitoring

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:15.110
available in Stackdriver.

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:17.590
And with the
Stackdriver integration,

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:20.360
a lot more of the metrics
are available through

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.580
the Stackdriver dashboard.

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:25.250
As you can see, if you
go into Stackdriver,

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:28.640
you can see all the key
metrics that you need

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:30.350
to monitor a Redis instance.

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:34.120
As you can see, Average Commands
Per Second, the 95th Percentile

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.190
Latency, Cache Hit Ratio, et
cetera, are all available.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:38.960
And these are important
metrics that you

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:43.340
need to determine how your
instance is functioning.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:46.220
If you want to see the full
list of all the metrics

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:49.610
that are available, just go
to Metrics Explorer, type

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:52.910
in Redis, and you can see
the full list of metrics that

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:55.780
are published in Stackdriver.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:58.700
So as you can see,
with Cloud Memorystore,

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:03.980
creating, managing, and
monitoring a Redis instance

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:07.130
is actually very easy.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:09.230
So in short, with
Cloud Memorystore

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:12.230
you don't have to worry
about maintaining your own VM

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:14.600
to manage your Redis instance.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:17.270
Instead, you can simply
click a few buttons

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:21.420
and then create, manage,
and monitor the instance.

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:24.560
So in summary, let's take a
quick look at the offering.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:26.930
With Cloud Memorystore,
you have a basic tier

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:27.890
and a standard tier.

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:32.960
And within the tiers, you
also have memory tiers,

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:35.750
which give you different memory
sizes with different network

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:37.010
performance.

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:39.320
As you can see, the
M1 capacity tier

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:42.380
gives you 3 gigabits per
second, while M5 gives you

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:45.020
about 12 gigabits per second.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:47.300
Also, with respect
to pricing, you

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:49.940
get a wide range
of pricing options

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:53.460
that allows you to choose
the right tier for you.

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:56.450
So as you can see, basic
M1 is less than $0.05

00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:59.780
per gigabyte hour, and
the standard instance

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:01.220
are a little bit more.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:03.230
Basic tier is ideal
if you want to have

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:07.070
a low-cost, simple Redis cache.

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:10.420
[MUSIC PLAYING]

